---
export const prerender = true;

import { Icon } from "astro-icon/components";
import IconButton from "../lib/IconButton.astro";
---

<html lang="en">
  <head>
    <meta charset="utf-8" />
    <meta name="viewport" content="width=device-width" />
    <title>Library author example</title>
  </head>
  <body>
    <h1><Icon name="icons:logo" size={24} /> Consumer app</h1>
    <p>
      This page belongs to the "consumer", but it imports and renders
      <code>&lt;IconButton&gt;</code> from <code>src/lib/</code> - a stand-in
      for a component library like <code>acme-ui</code>. See
      <code>src/content.config.ts</code> for the one line that wires the
      library's icons in: <code>...acmeUiIcons</code>.
    </p>

    <section>
      <h2>A library component, used normally</h2>
      <IconButton icon="chevron-right">Next</IconButton>
      <IconButton icon="close">Dismiss</IconButton>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>UC4: styling an icon's internals</h2>
      <p>
        <code>spinner.svg</code> has two paths meant to be styled
        independently (<code>.spinner-track</code>, <code>.spinner-head</code>).
        The first two buttons below both render it plain - two occurrences on
        one page is exactly what triggers dedup, so both collapse into one
        shared <code>&lt;symbol&gt;</code> referenced by <code>&lt;use&gt;</code>.
        CSS in <em>this</em> document can't reach past a <code>&lt;use&gt;</code>
        into that shared content, so `IconButton.astro`'s
        <code>.spinner-head</code> animation never reaches them. The third
        button passes <code>animateIcon</code>, which the library forwards to
        <code>&lt;Icon inline&gt;</code> - opting that one usage out of sprite
        optimization entirely, so its own <code>.spinner-head</code> stays
        real, local markup the animation can actually target.
      </p>
      <IconButton icon="spinner">Loading (sprited, not animated)</IconButton>
      <IconButton icon="spinner">Loading (sprited, not animated)</IconButton>
      <IconButton icon="spinner" animateIcon>Loading (inline, animated)</IconButton>
    </section>

    <section>
      <h2>Using a library icon directly</h2>
      <p>
        Consumers aren't limited to <code>&lt;IconButton&gt;</code> - any
        icon the library ships is addressable the normal way, with the
        collection key as its namespace:
      </p>
      <Icon name="acme-ui-icons:chevron-right" size={24} />
    </section>
  </body>
</html>
